Spec: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/spec.md (Tee, Memory bound; AC8, supports AC11, AC12, AC24, AC61)
Research: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/research.md (Q12: Budget.Peak)
Why this approach: Budget is lock-free (CAS loop on inUse, CAS max on peak); both tees of one exchange share an unexported reservation (mutex, bytes held, dropped flag), so a refusal on either goroutine hands back the whole exchange at once and both tees stop copying. A shared bodyCopy checks max_body_bytes before reserving, so a truncated body never reserves past the cap and is never dropped for memory. teeReader reads outside its mutex so seal never waits on a blocked read; teeWriter copies only what the inner Write accepted, after it returns.
Alternatives rejected: per-tee reservations (a refusal could not release the other tee's bytes, as plan step 3 requires); holding the mutex across the inner Read (seal would block on a stalled client body); wrapping http.NoBody (the transport would send chunked framing for an empty body).
Trade-off / known limit: after the other tee's refusal, a tee's chunk slices stay reachable until its next Write/Read or seal, though their bytes are already returned to the budget. A 1xx WriteHeader is forwarded but not recorded. Recorded in plan.md.
Verified by: make verify; go test -race ./internal/core (TestBudget_ReserveRelease, TestBudget_Peak, TestTeeReader_CopiesAndCaps, TestTeeReader_SealRecordsEOF, TestTeeWriter_Unwrap, TestTeeWriter_CopiesStatusAndHeaders, TestCapture_OutgoingRequestHasNoGetBody); tests first failed to build on the missing symbols; Peak, Unwrap and GetBody guards each proved by breaking the code for a moment (dropping raisePeak, Unwrap returning nil, setting GetBody) and seeing them fail, then reverted.
